# What NOT to upload to EAS Build.
#
# Two problems this solves, both measured 2026-08-10:
#
# 1. SIZE. The archive was 955 MB — 58s to compress and 1m15s to upload on
#    EVERY build. `android/app/build` alone is 540 MB of Gradle output.
#
# 2. WORKFLOW CONSISTENCY. `mobile/android/` exists on this machine but
#    `mobile/ios/` does not, so EAS saw a BARE project for Android and a
#    MANAGED one for iOS — two different flows per platform, from one repo.
#    Worse, EAS rejects a `runtimeVersion` *policy* in the bare workflow, which
#    is what broke a build here before.
#
#    `android/` is untracked (excluded in .git/info/exclude) and gitignored, so
#    it is a local prebuild artifact by definition — no other machine and no CI
#    run has ever had it, and nothing can depend on it. Excluding it makes EAS
#    prebuild BOTH platforms from app.json + app.config.js, identically.
#
#    app.config.js is what injects GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY into both native shapes
#    (ios.config.googleMapsApiKey and android.config.googleMaps.apiKey), so the
#    generated projects get the key the same way on both platforms.
#
# If you ever add a genuine native customisation, it belongs in an Expo config
# plugin or app.config.js — NOT in a hand-edited android/, which is invisible to
# git and vanishes on the next prebuild.

# Native prebuild artifacts — regenerated by EAS
android/
ios/

# Dependencies — EAS installs from the lockfile
node_modules/

# Build outputs and caches
dist/
.expo/
.expo-shared/
web-build/

# Tests and coverage — never needed to build the app
coverage/
**/__tests__/
**/__snapshots__/

# Local env — EAS uses its own environment variables (`eas env:list`)
.env
.env.local
